<h2>Hail Season in Central Texas</h2>
<p>Central Texas sits on the southern edge of the most active hail region in the country. Spring is the main hail season here — storms build along drylines and fronts from roughly March through June, with April and May usually the busiest months — but hail can fall any time thunderstorms are strong enough. The Austin metro’s rapid growth means more roofs sit in the path of each storm, and a single supercell tracking along I-35 or across the Hill Country can damage thousands of homes in an afternoon.</p>
<p>Because storms are so localized, your neighbor across the highway may see nothing while your street gets golf-ball hail. That is one reason we recommend an inspection after any storm that drops hail around one inch or larger (about quarter-size) — the size where shingle damage becomes common — or after any storm where you see dented gutters, cars or patio furniture.</p>

<h2>Signs of Hail Damage on Your Roof</h2>
<p>Some hail damage is obvious. Most is not. Here is what we look for on an Austin hail damage roof repair inspection:</p>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Bruised shingles</h3><p>Soft spots that feel spongy when pressed, where the hail fractured the mat beneath the surface. These often look fine from a ladder.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Granule loss</h3><p>Dark, round marks where hail knocked off the protective granules, exposing asphalt to the Texas sun. Piles of granules in gutters or at downspouts are a clue.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Cracked or split shingles</h3><p>Large hail and older, brittle shingles produce clean cracks or broken corners that let water in directly.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Dented metal</h3><p>Dimples in metal roofing, ridge vents, flashing, gutters and downspouts — useful evidence of hail size even when the roof surface is debated.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Damaged vents &amp; boots</h3><p>Cracked plastic vents, split pipe boots and dented turbines that can leak long before the shingles do.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Collateral clues</h3><p>Damaged window screens, AC fins, fences and siding help confirm the storm’s path and intensity for your insurer.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Stay off the roof.</strong> Wet, granule-covered shingles are slippery and hail-damaged decking can be weak. Note the date and time of the storm, photograph hail on the ground next to a coin or ruler if it is safe, and leave the climbing to a roofer.</p></div>

<h2>How Hail Damages Different Roof Types</h2>
