<h2>Hail Damage and Your Insurance Claim</h2>
<p>Hail is a sudden, covered peril under most Texas homeowners policies, but how much a claim pays depends on your policy type, deductible and the documented condition of your roof. Many Texas policies carry a percentage-based wind and hail deductible, and some pay actual cash value rather than replacement cost on older roofs. Read your policy’s declarations page so there are no surprises.</p>
<p>Austin Roofing’s role is to give you and your insurer accurate, photo-backed facts about the damage. We are not public adjusters and we do not negotiate your claim — and under Texas law, no contractor may waive or rebate your deductible. For a full walk-through of claims, see our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claims/">roof insurance claim help</a> page and our guide to <a href="/blog/insurance-coverage-for-storm-damaged-roofs/">insurance coverage for storm-damaged roofs</a>.</p>

<h2>Repair or Replace After Hail?</h2>
<p>Scattered hits on a younger roof can often be repaired by replacing individual shingles. When damage is spread across most slopes, when the roof is older and brittle, or when matching shingles are no longer available, replacement usually makes more sense — and insurers often agree when the damage is well documented. Our <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">roof replacement</a> page explains what that looks like, and many homeowners use the opportunity to upgrade to impact-resistant shingles, which some insurers reward with premium discounts.</p>

<h2>Why Prompt Hail Repair Matters</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Bruised shingles lose granules faster in Austin’s UV, turning hidden damage into leaks</li>
  <li>Your policy may require you to report damage within a set time and to prevent further damage</li>
  <li>Fresh damage is easier to tie to a specific storm date</li>
  <li>Small leaks caught early avoid wet decking, insulation and drywall</li>
</ul>
